{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== HANDOFF #9 — epic e369 (Jira Server/Data Center). READ THIS, THEN #8, THEN #7. ===\n\nWORKTREE /Users/joeoakhart/rebar-jira-family, branch jira-family-seam.\nALWAYS: export PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\"\n\n*** STATUS: ALL FOUR SHIPPED-CODE DEFECTS ARE CLOSED. J11's SLICE IS 25/26 GREEN. THE OUTBOUND\nHALF OF THE HEADLINE AC IS STILL UNPROVEN — 5200 REMAINS THE EPIC'S CLOSING CONDITION. ***\n\nCLOSED EARLIER THIS SESSION: 9263 (plan_defect), 21fc, 387d, 7c26 (all preexisting).\nMERGED: 20 changes (1129/1130/1131/1136, 1150-1153, 1158/1159/1161/1162/1163/1165/1166/1168/1172).\nIN REVIEW: 1177 (needs votes) — the latest J11 test fix.\n\nEPIC CHILDREN: 5200 (J11, in_progress, claimed), 6a91, cc77, fe15, b586 (+ its two new children).\n\n--- J11 (5200) — claimed, plan PASSED after 12 rounds ---\n25 of 26 live cells green against a real DC instance. PROVEN: isolation on BOTH repos (the outer\none AND the `.tickets-tracker` STORE repo, which is the one `sync.remote` would push); a complete\nscrubbed copy of the real store, count-matched; zero deletions AND zero outbound updates over it;\nthe inbound create PLANNED; and the inbound round-trip APPLIED. NOT PROVEN: the outbound\nassertion — a local edit surfacing on the DC issue. DO NOT CLOSE THE EPIC WITHOUT IT.\n\nNEXT ACTION FOR J11: land 1177, then run the harness\n(`gh workflow run external-integration.yml --repo navapbc/rebar --ref main`) and read\n`test_a_local_edit_reaches_the_dc_issue_outbound`. Everything upstream of it is green and\n`bound_dc_issue` asserts the binding before yielding, so a failure isolates cleanly to the\noutbound apply path.\n\n--- b586 IS SPLIT (this session), and the split is already vindicated ---\n  * [rebar:f2f3-9cb1-335b-4e31] S1 — extract the retry/error cluster out of `transport.py`.\n    Behaviour-preserving; FORCED by the cap (789/800 lines). **PASSED plan review FIRST TRY** —\n    b586 itself took 10 rounds. Attestation CERTIFIED.\n  * [rebar:6758-26b8-c9ea-4c5d] S2 — the 429 policy in the extracted module. `depends_on` S1 as a\n    real link (verified: `next-batch` withholds S2 while S1 is open).\n  Both retyped from story to TASK, because `next-batch` reports a childless story as\n  NEEDS_PLANNING and it would never have been offered as workable.\n\nS1 IS CERTIFIED BUT NOT YET CLAIMABLE: the parent-first cascade needs b586 itself in_progress, and\nb586 has no attestation. b586's description has been REWRITTEN as a parent/decomposition plan (its\n28k-char implementation plan now lives in the children). Its review returned BLOCK with ONE finding\n— that S2 left the `_paged_search` opt-in shape ambiguous — and THAT IS NOW FIXED: S2 decides it\n(`accepts and forwards rate_limit_retry: bool = False`; an unconditional True would silently grant\nretry to every future caller). **NEXT ACTION: re-run `rebar review-plan b586-2fda-8a15-4260`.**\nOnce it certifies: claim b586, then claim S1, then implement S1.\n\n--- PARALLELISM: WHAT IS ACTUALLY SAFE (from `rebar next-batch e369-a449-4773-48fb`) ---\nUnblocked and file-disjoint from J11 (which is TEST-ONLY, `tests/external/live_jira_dc/`):\n  fe15  -> `adapters/jira/*` (CLOUD adapter). Disjoint.\n  6a91  -> `_backend.py`, contract tests. Disjoint.\n  S1    -> `jira_datacenter/transport.py` -> `retry.py`. Disjoint.\nDO NOT run cc77 alongside S1. `next-batch`'s conflict matrix shows all-dots, but that is a FALSE\nNEGATIVE: cc77's recorded impact names only `test_mypy_ratchet.py` while its actual subject is\n\"transport params are untyped\" — it will touch `transport.py`, S1's file. Serialize: S1 first, then\ncc77 against the extracted module.\nA HARNESS RUN IS NOT AN LLM GATE, so a plan review can overlap one. Plan reviews contend with EACH\nOTHER (and with the concurrent session in /Users/joeoakhart/rebar-wt-provider-seam) — run one at a\ntime.\n\n--- THE DEBUG PROTOCOL RESULT (the outbound blocker, root-caused) ---\n`rebar edit` against the store copy failed \"ticket system not initialized\". ROOT CAUSE, confirmed\nat RUNTIME by instrumentation: the write seam's `.env-id` gate (`_seam.py:374-381` — \"every write\nflows through here ... the backstop none can bypass\"). `.env-id` is the FIRST line of the tickets\nbranch's own `.gitignore`, so `git archive` can NEVER contain it. Fixed with `run_ensures`\n(rebar's ensure-registry), the sanctioned remedy — `infra/scripts/reviewbot-ensure-tickets.sh`\ndoes the same for a fresh clone (bug d220).\nTWO OF MY HYPOTHESES WERE WRONG FIRST (REBAR_ROOT; `canonical_tracker`) because my initial grep for\nthe message was truncated at `head -5` — there are FOURTEEN sites. And TWO guards share that one\nstring with DIFFERENT conditions: `event_append` wants `.git`, the seam wants `.env-id`. The\nreconciler writes via the former and the library via the latter, so THE RECONCILER SUCCEEDS ON THE\nVERY STORE A LIBRARY EDIT REFUSES. Reasoning from \"the reconciler wrote, so the store is\ninitialized\" is what hid it. `tests/unit/test_store_copy_needs_env_id.py` pins both halves.\n\n--- STANDING RULES ADDED THIS SESSION (the earlier ones in #6/#7/#8 still hold) ---\n * SYNC → REVIEW → CLAIM. The reviewer reads `origin/main`; an attestation signed while your\n   worktree sits on an unmerged commit is `stale-head` and the claim is refused. Check cheaply with\n   `rebar review-plan <id> --status` (read-only, no LLM).\n * A `rebar edit`/`comment` CAN FAIL ON LOCK CONTENTION while the script still reports OK\n   (`flock: could not acquire lock after 60s`). Always re-read and compare LENGTH after a write.\n * `pytest --collect-only` PROVES NOTHING ABOUT FIXTURES — they resolve at SETUP. Use\n   `pytest --fixtures <module>`.\n * THE GITHUB MIRROR LAGS GERRIT. After a submit, verify content against `git fetch gerrit main`\n   and wait for the mirror before dispatching a workflow that runs `--ref main`.\n * DON'T ENUMERATE STORE DOT-FILES. Ticket ids never start with a dot; filter structurally. An\n   enumerated list miscounts the moment the store gains a marker (this broke the completeness cell\n   the moment `run_ensures` started creating `.env-id`).\n * PIN A BASELINE YOU COMPARE AGAINST. The tickets branch is LIVE (auto-push on every write, plus a\n   concurrent session), so re-fetching it at assertion time samples a different commit than the one\n   you copied."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785617540322218001,"uuid":"5441b52d-bff8-48d7-b0e3-ef328877e40e"}